## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of memory layout, overlap handling, and cache-friendly copy strategies in tight, real-world paths.

## Key Concepts
- Overlap-safe memmove
- Cache-friendly 64-byte blocks
- Alignment assumptions and tail handling
- Fallback to memcpy for tiny or unaligned data
- Microbenchmark plan to measure throughput vs standard memcpy

## Code Example
```c
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>

void *memmove64(void *dst, const void *src, size_t n){
  unsigned char *d = (unsigned char*)dst;
  const unsigned char *s = (const unsigned char*)src;
  const size_t BLOCK = 64;

  if ((size_t)(d) <= (size_t)(s) || d >= s + n){
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      d += BLOCK; s += BLOCK; n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d, s, n);
  } else {
    d += n; s += n;
    while (n >= BLOCK){
      d -= BLOCK; s -= BLOCK;
      memcpy(d, s, BLOCK);
      n -= BLOCK;
    }
    if (n) memcpy(d - n, s - n, n);
  }
  return dst;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for arbitrary alignment and larger data sizes?
- Compare performance to builtin memmove on x86-64 with different runtimes.
- What edge cases or architectures could break this approach?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of lock-free design, cross-socket contention, and memory ordering on MESI-based systems.

## Key Concepts
- Per-core local buffers to minimize cross-socket traffic and reduce coherence domain contention
- Per-slot sequence counters to prevent ABA race conditions
- Correct memory ordering: release for publishing, acquire for consuming, ensuring proper visibility across threads
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Wrap-around handling via power-of-two masking combined with sequence counter verification
- Backpressure mechanisms to handle slow consumers gracefully

## Data Structures

```c
typedef struct {
    uint64_t seq;
    char pad[64];  // align to cache line boundary
    void* data;
    char payload[112];  // 128-byte slots total
} Slot;

typedef struct {
    Slot slots[1 << 18];
    _Atomic(uint64_t) head;
    _Atomic(uint64_t) tail;
    char pad[L1_CACHE_SIZE];
} Ring;

typedef struct {
    char buffer[1 << 12][128];
    uint64_t seq[1 << 12];
    uint32_t local_head;
    char pad[L1_CACHE_SIZE];
} PerCoreQueue;
```

## Pseudocode

**Enqueue:**
```c
bool enqueue(Ring* ring, PerCoreQueue* local, void* data, size_t len) {
    uint32_t slot = local->local_head & (1 << 12 - 1);

    memcpy(local->buffer[slot], data, len);
    atomic_store_explicit(&local->seq[slot], 
                          local->seq[slot] + 2, 
                          memory_order_relaxed);

    uint64_t desired = local->local_head + 1;
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
            &ring->tail, &desired, desired,
            memory_order_release, memory_order_relaxed)) {
        local->local_head++;
        return true;
    }
    return false;  // backpressure
}
```

**Dequeue:**
```c
bool dequeue(Ring* ring, void* out, size_t* len) {
    uint64_t head = atomic_load_explicit(&ring->head, memory_order_relaxed);

    uint64_t desired = head + 1;
    if (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
            &ring->head, &head, desired,
            memory_order_acquire, memory_order_relaxed)) {
        return false;
    }

    Slot* slot = &ring->slots[head & (N - 1)];
    uint64_t seq = atomic_load_explicit(&slot->seq, memory_order_acquire);
    if ((seq & 1) != 0) return false;  // not yet written

    memcpy(out, slot->data, *len);
    atomic_store_explicit(&slot->seq, seq + 1, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}
```

## Microbenchmark Plan

1. **Throughput measurement:** Vary producer/consumer thread counts (1-16), measure ops/sec at fixed load.

2. **Cache-coherence traffic:** Use perf counters (`perf stat -e cache-references,cache-misses, LLC-load-misses, LLC-store-misses`) across:
   - Single-socket baseline (no cross-socket traffic)
   - Multi-socket with threads pinned to different NUMA nodes
   - Vary write-to-read ratios (100/0, 50/50, 0/100)

3. **Contention stress test:** Saturate ring capacity, measure under overflow conditions.

4. **Baseline comparison:** Compare against mutex-protected ring and lock-free SPSC queue.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of non-blocking data structures, ABA avoidance, memory ordering, and NUMA-aware performance under mixed-priority traffic. It also touches on backpressure and practical microbenchmark design.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free design and ABA avoidance with per-slot sequence counters
- Memory ordering: release on enqueue, acquire on dequeue
- NUMA-aware layout and cache-line alignment
- Priority routing, backpressure metrics, and spillover handling
- Correct wrap-around handling and bounded capacity

## Code Example
```javascript
// Ring buffer with 64-bit sequence per slot
struct Slot {
    alignas(64) uint8_t data[64];
    std::atomic<uint64_t> seq;
};

class Ring {
    Slot buffer[N];
    std::atomic<uint64_t> head{0}, tail{0};

public:
    bool enqueue(const void* payload) {
        uint64_t pos = tail.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        uint64_t seq = buffer[pos & mask].seq.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        if (seq != pos) return false; // full or wrap-around
        memcpy(buffer[pos & mask].data, payload, 64);
        buffer[pos & mask].seq.store(pos + 1, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    bool dequeue(void* payload) {
        uint64_t pos = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        uint64_t seq = buffer[pos & mask].seq.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
        if (seq != pos + 1) return false; // empty
        memcpy(payload, buffer[pos & mask].data, 64);
        buffer[pos & mask].seq.store(pos + N + 1, std::memory_order_release);
        head.store(pos + 1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
        return true;
    }
};

// Two rings per priority, producer selects based on flag
```

## Performance Measurement
- **Latency**: Use RDTSC/RDTSCP around enqueue/dequeue, measure percentiles under mixed priority ratios (90/10, 50/50)
- **Backpressure**: Count enqueue failures per ring, track queue depth, measure producer stall time
- **NUMA**: Pin threads to sockets, allocate buffers locally, measure remote memory penalties
- **Throughput**: Vary producer/consumer ratios, monitor cache miss rates with perf

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical concurrency on bare-metal MCUs: interrupt-safe data sharing, wrap-around handling, and overflow behavior in a tight, deterministic path.

## Key Concepts
- Circular buffers with power-of-two sizing
- Volatile indices and memory visibility between ISR and main code
- Minimal interrupt masking to avoid latency spikes
- Edge-case testing for wrap and overflow

## Code Example
```c
#define N 32
#define MASK (N-1)
static uint8_t buf[N];
static volatile uint8_t head = 0; // consumer index
static volatile uint8_t tail = 0; // producer index

int enqueue_byte(uint8_t b) {
  uint8_t next = (tail + 1) & MASK;
  if (next == head) return -1; // full
  buf[tail] = b;
  // memory barrier if available
  tail = next;
  return 0;
}

int dequeue_byte(uint8_t *out) {
  if (head == tail) return -1; // empty
  *out = buf[head];
  head = (head + 1) & MASK;
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for multi-producer/multi-consumer safely?
- What tests would you run on a simulated interrupt workload to prove correctness?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical low-level memory management in NUMA systems, emphasizing locality, contention, and false sharing—key for latency-sensitive trading/analytics workloads.

## Key Concepts

- NUMA locality and per-node free lists
- Lock-free/atomic free-list operations and ABA avoidance
- Cross-node migration policies and migration costs
- Cache-line alignment and false sharing avoidance
- Validation: microbenchmarks with mixed-local/global allocations, perf measurements

## Code Example

```cpp
// simplified per-node free-list CAS skeleton
#include <atomic>

struct Node {
  std::atomic<void*> head{nullptr};
};

void* alloc(Node& node) {
  void* h = node.head.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  if (h) {
    void* next = *(void**)h;
    if (node.head.compare_exchange_weak(h, next, std::memory_order_acquire, std::memory_order_relaxed))
      return h;
  }
  // fallback: steal from other node or central pool
  return nullptr;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement ABA protection and reclaim memory safely across NUMA nodes?
- How would you measure cross-node traffic impact and optimize for a workload with hot/cold object lifetimes?
